  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 137 PM CDT Sat Sep 5 2026\n\nAfter an unseasonably hot and humid stretch to start September,\ncooler conditions are beginning to filter over the area from \nnorth to south today. Moisture pooling along a cold front draped\nacross the southern CWA combined with temps in the lower 90s \nsouth of the front is leading to heat index values around 105F. \nThose numbers have likely peaked as the front should clear the \nremainder of the CWA over the next couple hours. While surface \nconvergence is fairly weak on the front, interactions with \nresidual outflow boundaries from earlier convection over Indiana\nare providing an added focus early this afternoon. Cannot rule \nout an isolated strong to briefly severe storm through 2-3pm \ngiven a weakly capped, highly unstable, and slightly sheared \nenvironment.\n\nSurface high pressure will expand across the Great Lakes region\nSunday and Monday. Though temps will remain seasonably warm, \ndrier air will advect in from the east through the day Sunday, \nwith values falling into the 50s during the afternoon and \ncontinuing through Monday.\n\nMid-level ridging will build back across the western Great \nLakes by Tuesday before deamplifying in response to a strong \nmid-level system crossing southern Canada on Wednesday. Daytime \nhighs both days will push well into the 80s to perhaps near 90, \nbut dew points are expected to be lower (mid to upper 60s) than \nthe past few days.\n\nA cold front associated with the southern Canadian system will \nslowly drag across the area Wednesday into early Thursday \nmorning. Minimal deep-layer forcing and weak low-level \nconvergence on the front suggest that the coverage of showers \nand embedded thunderstorms will be, at most, scattered. Would \nnot be surprised to see a mostly dry frontal passage.\n\nLike Sunday and Monday, cooler (but seasonably warm) conditions\nwill follow the front late in the week before ridging again \nbuilds over the area and results in unseasonably warm conditions\nnext weekend.\n\nKluber",
